{"term":"libertino","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"libertine","concept":"libertine","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":30,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A man who freely indulges in sensual pleasures without moral restraint; a sexual libertine.","english_term":"libertine","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Direct calque from French 'libertin'; used in historical and literary contexts; retains ironic elegance in modern Italian, especially in Renaissance and Enlightenment scholarship."}
{"term":"libidinoso","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"lasciviousness","concept":"lasciviousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":50,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Driven by or expressing excessive sexual desire; lusting, libidinous.","english_term":"libidinous","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Derives from Latin 'libido'; more formal and literary than 'lascivo'; commonly deployed in journalism and literary criticism to describe lewd behavior without resorting to cruder alternatives."}
{"term":"pruriginoso","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"prurience","concept":"pruriency","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":25,"technical":40,"recognition_pct_current":45,"recognition_pct_peak":45,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Having or provoking a prurient, itching interest in sexual matters; salacious.","english_term":"prurient","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primary medical sense is 'causing itching' (from 'prurigo'); the sexual/prurient sense is a secondary literary-legal register; most Italian speakers would hear the dermatological meaning first and the sexual meaning only in context."}
{"term":"libido","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"sexual drive","concept":"lust","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":10,"technical":85,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexual drive or desire; the Freudian psychoanalytic concept of libidinal energy.","english_term":"libido","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Italian borrowed the term unchanged from Latin via Freudian psychoanalysis; used clinically and in popular health discourse; carries little vulgarity precisely because of its clinical pedigree."}
{"term":"pulsione sessuale","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"sexual drive","concept":"lust","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":80,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The sexual drive or instinct; the Italian psychoanalytic rendering of Freud's 'Trieb' applied to sexuality.","english_term":"sexual drive","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Pulsione' is the standard Italian translation of Freud's 'Trieb'; 'pulsione sessuale' is used primarily in clinical and academic contexts; feels technical and neutral rather than erotic."}
{"term":"appetito sessuale","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"sexual desire","concept":"lust","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":15,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexual appetite; the colloquial idiom for sex drive or libido.","english_term":"sexual appetite","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Calques directly from English 'sexual appetite'; widely understood across all registers; appears in health journalism, self-help books, and casual conversation without strong vulgarity."}
{"term":"bramosia carnale","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"carnal desire","concept":"carnal_desire","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":35,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Intense carnal longing; a consuming sexual craving framed in literary or religious terms.","english_term":"carnal desire","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"'Bramosia' (yearning, craving) is elevated literary register; 'carnale' adds the religious-moral dimension; the phrase evokes Renaissance poetry and Dante; less common in contemporary informal speech but immediately understood."}
{"term":"voglie carnali","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"carnal desire","concept":"carnal_desire","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":30,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Carnal urges or desires (plural); fleshly sexual impulses, often invoked in moral or religious condemnation.","english_term":"carnal desires","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"'Voglie' (desires, cravings) in this construction specifically denotes sexual urges; the plural form suggests habitual or multiple impulses; appears frequently in religious texts and moralistic literature; slightly archaic in secular speech."}
{"term":"sfrenato","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"unrestrained","concept":"wanton_behavior","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":30,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":75,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Unbridled, unrestrained; applied to sexual conduct to mean wantonly licentious or morally uncontrolled.","english_term":"unbridled","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literally 'without brakes or reins' (from 'freno'); applies broadly to unchecked behavior in any domain; in sexual contexts implies moral abandon; commonly used with nouns like 'sessualità', 'desiderio', or 'vita' rather than standing alone."}
{"term":"dissoluto","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"debauchery","concept":"libertine","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":35,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Dissolute; morally unrestrained, given to excessive sensual indulgence and debauchery.","english_term":"dissolute","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Famous in Italian culture as the subtitle of Mozart's Don Giovanni ('il dissoluto punito'); registers as literary and cultural; implies broader moral dissipation beyond sexuality alone; recognized by educated speakers across generations."}
{"term":"depravato","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"depraved","concept":"debauchery","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":55,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Depraved; morally corrupt and sexually perverted.","english_term":"depraved","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"More strongly pejorative than 'dissoluto'; implies active moral corruption and sexual perversion; used in journalism and legal contexts to describe sex offenders or extreme moral failure; carries stronger condemnatory force than its English cognate."}
{"term":"vizioso","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"vice","concept":"libertine","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":40,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Given to vice; in sexual contexts, a person with deviant or excessive sexual appetites; can shade into contemporary informal 'kinky' or 'naughty'.","english_term":"vicious","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primary sense is 'full of vices or defects'; in sexual contexts the register ranges from literary 'debauched' to informal 'kinky'; English 'vicious' (violent) is a false cognate — 'vizioso' carries no connotation of violence."}
{"term":"lascivia","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"lasciviousness","concept":"lasciviousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":40,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Lasciviousness; sexual wantonness and licentiousness.","english_term":"lasciviousness","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"From Latin 'lascivia'; used in literary, historical, and religious texts; slightly archaic in everyday speech but recognized by educated speakers; overlaps with 'lussuria' but carries a more playful, less sinful connotation — lust as sport rather than sin."}
{"term":"lussuria","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"lust","concept":"lust","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":35,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Lust; one of the seven deadly sins in Catholic theology; intense, morally condemned sexual desire.","english_term":"lust","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Heavily loaded with Catholic moral theology; the lustful occupy the second circle of Dante's Inferno; widely recognized; in modern Italian can be used secularly but retains strong religious overtones that distinguish it from the more clinical 'libido'."}
{"term":"frigido","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"sexual frigidity","concept":"sexual_frigidity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":25,"technical":40,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Frigid (masculine form); lacking sexual desire or responsiveness; sexually cold and unresponsive, applied to a man.","english_term":"frigid","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Masculine form; less culturally loaded than the feminine 'frigida' because the concept of male frigidity is less socially salient in Italian culture; also used as a general insult for an emotionally cold person regardless of sexuality."}
{"term":"frigida","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"sexual frigidity","concept":"sexual_frigidity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":30,"technical":35,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Frigid (feminine form); a woman who lacks sexual desire or is unresponsive to sexual stimulation.","english_term":"frigid","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The feminine form is far more common and culturally loaded than the masculine; historically used as both a clinical term and an insult against women who refused or did not respond to male advances; now widely recognized as sexist but still in circulation."}
{"term":"impotente","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"erectile dysfunction","concept":"erectile_dysfunction","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":35,"technical":40,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Impotent; unable to achieve or maintain an erection; also used figuratively for powerlessness or helplessness.","english_term":"impotent","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Has both the sexual sense (erectile dysfunction) and the general political-social sense (powerless, unable to act); the sexual meaning is universally understood; clinically supplanted by 'disfunzione erettile' but 'impotente' remains in common use as a slur attacking male virility."}
{"term":"anorgasmico","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"anorgasmia","concept":"sexual_frigidity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":90,"recognition_pct_current":40,"recognition_pct_peak":40,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Anorgasmic; clinically unable to achieve orgasm despite adequate sexual stimulation.","english_term":"anorgasmic","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Strictly clinical; rarely used outside sexological or medical contexts; the concept is more commonly expressed colloquially as 'non riuscire ad avere un orgasmo'; the noun 'anorgasmia' is somewhat more common than this adjectival form."}
{"term":"castrato","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"castration","concept":"emasculation","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":50,"technical":45,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A castrated man; historically a male singer castrated before puberty to preserve a high vocal range; in sexual contexts, any castrated male.","english_term":"castrato","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"English borrowed the word unchanged from Italian; in Italian the term carries both its historical musical meaning (the castrati of 17th–18th century baroque opera) and its general sexual-insult meaning attacking male virility; the musical register lends the word a peculiar cultural double life."}
{"term":"casto","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"chastity","concept":"sexual_abstinence","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":0,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Chaste; sexually pure and abstinent, especially as a moral or religious virtue.","english_term":"chaste","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Positive moral term in Catholic tradition; contrasts directly with 'lussurioso'; applied to those who abstain from sex outside marriage or who take vows of celibacy; can be used with slight irony in secular contexts to describe someone unexpectedly restrained."}
{"term":"pudico","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"modesty","concept":"prudishness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":0,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Modest, bashful, or decorous about sexual matters; having a sense of sexual shame or propriety.","english_term":"prudish","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From Latin 'pudor' (shame, modesty); unlike English 'prudish', 'pudico' in Italian tends toward the positive — appropriate decorum rather than neurotic repression; more literary than everyday; the noun 'pudore' (sense of shame, modesty) is more commonly used."}
{"term":"bacchettone","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"prudishness","concept":"prudishness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":10,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A piously prudish person; someone who is publicly moralistic and sexually censorious, often implying underlying hypocrisy.","english_term":"prude","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From 'bacchetta' (small rod), originally referencing flagellant penitents; now a distinctly Italian cultural type combining sanctimonious religiosity with sexual prudishness and an implication of hypocrisy; no single English word captures this specific blend — 'prude' misses the hypocrite dimension, 'sanctimonious' misses the sexual dimension."}
{"term":"bigotto","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"religious prudishness","concept":"sexual_conservatism","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":10,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A religious bigot or moral prude; someone excessively devoted to religious observance and moralistic about others' sexual behavior.","english_term":"bigot","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"While English 'bigot' has shifted to primarily denoting racial or ethnic intolerance, Italian 'bigotto' retains the original meaning of religious zealotry and sexual prudishness; overlaps with 'bacchettone' but emphasizes dogmatic intolerance over personal hypocrisy."}
{"term":"puritano","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"puritanism","concept":"sexual_conservatism","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":5,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A puritan; a person with strict moral standards especially regarding sexuality; one who is sexually repressed or puritanical.","english_term":"puritan","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Borrowed from English Puritanism; used primarily as a secular adjective or noun meaning excessively moralistic and sexually repressed; in Italian the word often implies an Anglophone cultural import, distancing the concept from native Italian Catholic prudishness."}
{"term":"moralizzatore","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"moralizing","concept":"sexual_conservatism","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":5,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A moralizer; a person who habitually lectures others about sexual or moral propriety.","english_term":"moralizer","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Generally pejorative in modern Italian; implies someone who imposes moral judgments on others' sexual conduct; used extensively in political and media commentary; the feminine form 'moralizzatrice' is also current."}
